// results resting on theory outside these graphs. `area` is the three-character MSC class as in data/concepts.js,
// `msc5` the five-character class. The area "basic" holds results whose first class lies outside 49 and that have no
// entry in their home folder yet: weak compactness in reflexive spaces, the Poincaré inequality and the
// Rellich–Kondrachov theorem (46, home folder ../../analysis/) and the mountain pass theorem (58E, no home folder yet).
// `concepts` are ids of data/concepts.js or carry the prefix of another folder.
// References to theorems of other folders: "st:" (supremum property), "ana:" (mean value theorem, fundamental theorem of
// calculus, Taylor, Picard–Lindelöf, implicit function theorem, Hahn–Banach, Banach–Alaoglu, projection theorem, …),
// "top:" (Poincaré lemma), "geo:" (isoperimetric inequality, separation theorem, Noether's theorem in Hamiltonian
// form); planned folder "de:" (Weyl's lemma).
//
// Prefix for references from other folders: "cv:<id>". Proposed anchors: fundamental-lemma,
// euler-lagrange-equation-theorem, direct-method, tonelli-existence, dirichlet-principle, noether-theorem-lagrangian,
// pontryagin-maximum-principle, douglas-rado-theorem, brenier-theorem.
// Sources: Gelfand and Fomin, Calculus of Variations (1963); Giaquinta and Hildebrandt, Calculus of Variations I (1996);
// Dacorogna, Introduction to the Calculus of Variations (3rd ed. 2015) and Direct Methods in the Calculus of Variations
// (2nd ed. 2008); Struwe, Variational Methods (4th ed. 2008); Dierkes, Hildebrandt and Sauvigny, Minimal Surfaces
// (2010); Braides, Γ-convergence for Beginners (2002); Villani, Topics in Optimal Transportation (2003); Fleming and
// Rishel, Deterministic and Stochastic Optimal Control (1975); Bardi and Capuzzo-Dolcetta, Optimal Control and
// Viscosity Solutions of Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman Equations (1997). Not checked by a specialist.
// Checked by ../../tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); calculating in the ordered field ℝ counts as using axioms, and so do the monotonicity
// and linearity of the integral, which are part of its definition.

